1
00:00:12,553 --> 00:00:14,972
{\an8}Poor soul.
What is he still doing up there?

2
00:00:15,139 --> 00:00:17,517
Waiting on the coroner.
Careful. Slow drip.

3
00:00:18,726 --> 00:00:20,102
A single shot through the back.

4
00:00:20,228 --> 00:00:22,063
Looks like he got off a few
rounds before he hit him.

5
00:00:22,188 --> 00:00:24,815
- 9-millimeter, maybe?
- It looks like. Was anybody here?

6
00:00:26,275 --> 00:00:28,903
Janitor came in at 7:00.
Says the victim's name is Jeremy Reese.

7
00:00:29,237 --> 00:00:31,197
- Lived here in Clearlake.
- Did the janitor see anyone?

8
00:00:31,447 --> 00:00:33,074
It was empty. It's a private gym.

9
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:34,992
Victim had a key,
but it's not in his bag.

10
00:00:35,201 --> 00:00:36,244
His car's still in the lot.

11
00:00:36,369 --> 00:00:38,746
Robbery, maybe?
We should go to his apartment now.

12
00:00:39,080 --> 00:00:40,456
- Have you seen Jane?
- Nope.

13
00:00:48,089 --> 00:00:51,884
- Good morning, Lisbon.
- Don't be so sure. We caught a case.

14
00:00:52,051 --> 00:00:55,304
- I've been alerted.
- "And yet you're not here in Clearlake."

15
00:00:55,471 --> 00:00:59,559
- Well, I'm busy.
- Jane. You can't obsess like this.

16
00:00:59,809 --> 00:01:03,145
Obsess?
I know that I've met Red John now.

17
00:01:03,271 --> 00:01:06,482
Shook his hand.
I have what you coppers call a lead.

18
00:01:06,691 --> 00:01:10,653
- Yes, but we have a fresh body here.
- I think you're obsessing.

19
00:01:10,861 --> 00:01:12,989
I will text you the address.
I'll see you there.

20
00:01:18,619 --> 00:01:21,914
Jeremy Reese. No record.
Not even a parking ticket.

21
00:01:22,331 --> 00:01:23,374
What do you think?

22
00:01:23,499 --> 00:01:27,920
I think he graduated from college
about three years ago. He's single.

23
00:01:28,379 --> 00:01:32,883
He works in the sciences.
Uh, maybe a biologist.

24
00:01:33,384 --> 00:01:36,095
He's from back east.
Likes puzzles.

25
00:01:36,554 --> 00:01:40,266
- No family in the area.
- Yeah. He was a botanist.

26
00:01:40,433 --> 00:01:43,227
He worked at a local pot farm
Westerly Organics.

27
00:01:43,436 --> 00:01:45,313
Pot farm? Oh, that follows.

28
00:01:45,438 --> 00:01:48,149
His last phone call was
to Alice Burns last night.

29
00:01:48,274 --> 00:01:50,109
She's an old college Professor of his.

30
00:01:50,234 --> 00:01:51,819
Yes, that would be her
in the photograph.

31
00:01:52,069 --> 00:01:54,447
I'm guessing the parents couldn't
make it out for the graduation.

32
00:01:54,572 --> 00:01:59,076
- Also this, um, apartment's been tossed.
- By who? Martha Stewart?

33
00:01:59,243 --> 00:02:01,996
No, by someone who was searching
for something of incredible value.

34
00:02:02,121 --> 00:02:04,957
They wanted to try and put the place
back together but they failed.

35
00:02:05,166 --> 00:02:07,752
Incredible value? There's about 200 bucks
worth of furniture in here.

36
00:02:08,377 --> 00:02:09,629
Allow me to show you.

37
00:02:11,213 --> 00:02:14,717
This plaid shirt here is
in amongst the stripes. Hard to miss.

38
00:02:16,802 --> 00:02:21,098
- That is a wall.
- So it is.

39
00:02:22,058 --> 00:02:25,061
Bear with me.
Eh...

40
00:02:28,397 --> 00:02:33,569
Yes. Uh, it's back over here.
There it is.

41
00:02:34,111 --> 00:02:36,113
See these two textbooks?
They're out of order.

42
00:02:36,322 --> 00:02:38,407
Volume one is next to volume three.

43
00:02:50,252 --> 00:02:52,129
- A safe.
- Found his keys.

44
00:02:55,424 --> 00:02:58,761
- It's empty.
- Killer got what they came for.

45
00:02:59,011 --> 00:03:01,764
We need to find someone
who knows what was in that safe.

46
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:05,017
I think this puzzle box is the key.

47
00:03:06,352 --> 00:03:09,397
Whatever's in here
was important enough to hide

48
00:03:09,689 --> 00:03:12,149
but not valuable enough
to put in that safe.

49
00:03:14,527 --> 00:03:15,569
What do you think it is?

50
00:03:15,695 --> 00:03:17,154
Well, we don't know yet.
It's a puzzle.

51
00:03:17,405 --> 00:03:19,323
- Cho, crack it open.
- Oh, no, no, no, no.

52
00:03:19,448 --> 00:03:22,410
It's my puzzle.
All mine.

53
00:03:43,904 --> 00:03:44,947
Hey, boss.

54
00:03:45,072 --> 00:03:46,156
We didn't get much
off Jeremy's phone.

55
00:03:46,406 --> 00:03:47,908
Mostly calls
to his family in Boston.

56
00:03:48,283 --> 00:03:49,910
Call his parents
and give them the bad news.

57
00:03:50,035 --> 00:03:52,079
Tell Rigsby to check
his e-mails and his texts.

58
00:03:52,204 --> 00:03:53,831
We need to find out
what was in that safe.

59
00:03:54,039 --> 00:03:55,082
I'm on it.

60
00:03:56,959 --> 00:03:59,962
Hello. I'm looking
for Agent Teresa Lisbon.

61
00:04:00,921 --> 00:04:05,133
{\an8}Mrs. Burns. You were the last person
Jeremy called before he was murdered.

62
00:04:07,469 --> 00:04:11,390
{\an8}- Do you need a moment?
- Uh, no, I'll be fine. I'm okay.

63
00:04:12,599 --> 00:04:17,187
{\an8}- Why did Jeremy call you?
- Just to talk. I was his advisor in college.

64
00:04:17,354 --> 00:04:18,564
Was he having problems?

65
00:04:19,106 --> 00:04:21,400
{\an8}He was upset over a disagreement
with his boss.

66
00:04:21,900 --> 00:04:26,905
{\an8}Not surprising if you know Matthew Gold.
A bit of a medical marijuana celebrity.

67
00:04:27,030 --> 00:04:29,324
{\an8}- He owns Westerly Organic?
- Yeah.

68
00:04:30,075 --> 00:04:32,035
Jeremy did not smoke, by the way.

69
00:04:32,244 --> 00:04:34,454
{\an8}Genetic research around marijuana
is simply

70
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:36,790
{\an8}the most exciting place
to be for a young botanist.

71
00:04:36,915 --> 00:04:38,667
{\an8}It was a good use of his skills.

72
00:04:38,876 --> 00:04:45,007
{\an8}- Did he go into specifics about the fight?
- No. He just said that he was anxious

73
00:04:45,132 --> 00:04:48,719
{\an8}over friction with his boss.
Jeremy could be...

74
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:53,974
{\an8}Secretive.
We're plant people.

75
00:04:54,182 --> 00:04:56,768
Did he have any enemies?
Anyone with an ax to grind?

76
00:04:56,894 --> 00:05:01,398
{\an8}No. He was kind
and generous to a fault.

77
00:05:01,523 --> 00:05:03,984
{\an8}Do you always keep in touch
with your students after graduation?

78
00:05:04,318 --> 00:05:09,448
{\an8}No. Jeremy's mother died
a few years ago

79
00:05:09,573 --> 00:05:13,201
{\an8}and with my son in Houston
we were a...

80
00:05:13,994 --> 00:05:15,370
Pretend family.

81
00:05:18,624 --> 00:05:19,750
{\an8}What are you sick with?

82
00:05:21,501 --> 00:05:24,755
{\an8}It started in my stomach
and spread to the lymph nodes.

83
00:05:25,756 --> 00:05:28,550
{\an8}School wants me to wrap it up.

84
00:05:28,967 --> 00:05:33,430
{\an8}I told them, "eh, one more semester."
He, um...

85
00:05:38,852 --> 00:05:39,895
{\an8}I'm sorry.

86
00:05:42,648 --> 00:05:46,443
{\an8}Um, brought me my prescription
every two weeks

87
00:05:46,568 --> 00:05:50,822
{\an8}and we would do the crossword.
He liked puzzles.

88
00:05:51,323 --> 00:05:53,241
{\an8}Any kind. Didn't matter.

89
00:05:53,408 --> 00:05:56,745
{\an8}- Your doctor lets you smoke?
- He prescribed it.

90
00:05:58,497 --> 00:06:00,374
{\an8}I'm dying.
It helps with the pain.

91
00:06:00,749 --> 00:06:02,459
{\an8}And helps me eat
my husband's cooking.

92
00:06:03,919 --> 00:06:05,837
{\an8}Uh, just... just
one more question...

93
00:06:06,922 --> 00:06:09,633
{\an8}you don't happen to know
how to open this, do you?

94
00:06:10,634 --> 00:06:15,013
{\an8}No. Jeremy made me one
and I forgot how it went.

95
00:06:15,138 --> 00:06:17,265
{\an8}Ah. Clever kid, huh?

96
00:06:35,325 --> 00:06:37,285
Hi, there.
We're with the CBI.

97
00:06:37,536 --> 00:06:39,705
We're looking for your boss,
Matthew gold.

98
00:06:39,955 --> 00:06:41,039
That's me.

99
00:06:42,082 --> 00:06:44,292
Can we talk in the barn?
I'm just hanging some buds.

100
00:06:45,836 --> 00:06:49,172
- Hangin' some buds.
- Well, someone's gotta do it.

101
00:06:50,632 --> 00:06:53,552
You know, I... I never had a kid
that smart on the farm.

102
00:06:54,928 --> 00:06:57,264
In five years, he'd... he'd have
been running this place.

103
00:06:57,389 --> 00:07:01,018
- Were any of your other workers jealous?
- No, everyone loved Jeremy.

104
00:07:01,435 --> 00:07:03,353
They'll all help plan a memorial.

105
00:07:04,813 --> 00:07:07,315
We're like minds here, you know?
Dedicated to organic growing.

106
00:07:07,441 --> 00:07:13,447
- None of that indoor chemical garbage.
- So, uh, how do I get involved?

107
00:07:13,572 --> 00:07:16,408
Do I have to get a prescription
or join a club or something?

108
00:07:16,533 --> 00:07:20,078
You'll be in the cooperative if you have
a condition we can help with.

109
00:07:20,537 --> 00:07:22,873
We, uh, we supply patients
all over the state.

110
00:07:24,249 --> 00:07:27,502
California says I can grow
no more than exactly 99 plants.

111
00:07:28,378 --> 00:07:31,381
Uncle Sam says
I'm a narco-terrorist.

112
00:07:31,798 --> 00:07:34,760
- I choose to side with California.
- Tough business.

113
00:07:34,885 --> 00:07:38,430
Eh, I just wish I could grow more.
But these laws...

114
00:07:39,181 --> 00:07:41,808
I'm no businessman.
Used to be.

115
00:07:41,933 --> 00:07:45,812
I gave up a software career to come
out here and make a difference.

116
00:07:46,813 --> 00:07:50,067
- We're healers, you know?
- Healers with an armed guard?

117
00:07:50,192 --> 00:07:54,154
Oh, Vick, yeah. He, uh, keeps
our intellectual property safe.

118
00:07:54,738 --> 00:07:58,575
We grow the dankest weed
in the valley thanks to these.

119
00:07:59,201 --> 00:08:02,204
"Dank"? I'm, uh,
I'm guessing that means "good"?

120
00:08:02,496 --> 00:08:04,247
Well, our patients like it.

121
00:08:06,875 --> 00:08:08,543
Jeremy helped me grow that crop.

122
00:08:10,212 --> 00:08:12,756
We won highest T.H.C.
at Mendocino Weed Wars.

123
00:08:13,548 --> 00:08:17,052
It's a cross between reclining Buddha
and some wicked Afghani stuff.

124
00:08:18,011 --> 00:08:20,263
I've, uh, turned down
offers from the cartels

125
00:08:20,388 --> 00:08:24,101
who don't exactly abide
by the social contract, hence Vick.

126
00:08:24,309 --> 00:08:25,811
When was the last time
you saw Jeremy?

127
00:08:25,977 --> 00:08:27,687
He was out of here
at 5:00 yesterday.

128
00:08:28,480 --> 00:08:29,981
Uh, the kids cook dinner together

129
00:08:30,107 --> 00:08:33,318
but, I don't know, Jeremy
usually did his own thing.

130
00:08:34,152 --> 00:08:36,571
Uh, we heard that Jeremy
was fighting with his boss.

131
00:08:36,738 --> 00:08:38,490
Fighting? No, no.

132
00:08:39,741 --> 00:08:43,411
We...we were eye to eye.
We were just planting next year's crop.

133
00:08:44,538 --> 00:08:46,832
- I can show you.
- Okay.

134
00:09:06,768 --> 00:09:07,811
Danky.

135
00:09:09,479 --> 00:09:12,858
Hi. I was hoping
you could help me.

136
00:09:13,859 --> 00:09:18,363
- Keep hoping.
- You're not scared of cops but, uh...

137
00:09:19,614 --> 00:09:21,616
you tried to avoid
eye contact back there.

138
00:09:22,951 --> 00:09:24,161
Because you're loyal to Jeremy?

139
00:09:24,286 --> 00:09:26,872
You think he was into something
that he shouldn't have been?

140
00:09:28,748 --> 00:09:30,375
- You're reaching.
- Always.

141
00:09:31,751 --> 00:09:33,503
Dippin' into
the company stash, huh?

142
00:09:33,712 --> 00:09:36,214
And don't deny it.
You're as high as a kite right now

143
00:09:36,339 --> 00:09:38,800
and I can guarantee you
that's against company policy.

144
00:09:39,926 --> 00:09:41,344
Big whoop.
So you know I'm baked.

145
00:09:41,761 --> 00:09:45,974
Oh, you're not just baked.
You are baked.

146
00:09:48,351 --> 00:09:50,478
- What's your name?
- Carson.

147
00:09:50,687 --> 00:09:54,399
What are you protecting
Jeremy from, Carson? He's dead.

148
00:09:58,278 --> 00:09:59,321
Okay.

149
00:10:00,780 --> 00:10:03,491
Like, six months back...
My car was jacked up in an accident.

150
00:10:03,617 --> 00:10:09,289
Jeremy gave me money to fix it.
8 g's. Like it was nothing.

151
00:10:09,873 --> 00:10:11,166
That's a lot of money.

152
00:10:11,583 --> 00:10:14,002
You think he was dealing
the marijuana on the side?

153
00:10:14,294 --> 00:10:17,255
Hey, I asked him.
He said, "hell no"

154
00:10:18,215 --> 00:10:19,341
I didn't care.

155
00:10:21,635 --> 00:10:25,347
But if Matthew found out,
he'd kill him.

156
00:10:25,764 --> 00:10:28,683
That's good to know.
Thank you, Carson.

157
00:10:29,893 --> 00:10:32,520
You need some more eye drops.
Just maybe a breath mint.

158
00:10:35,398 --> 00:10:36,775
Secret's safe with me.

159
00:10:38,008 --> 00:10:40,010
No big-ticket items
on Jeremy's credit card.

160
00:10:40,219 --> 00:10:41,470
I don't know
what Jane's talking about.

161
00:10:42,012 --> 00:10:43,222
Kid lived cheap.

162
00:10:43,581 --> 00:10:46,125
Yeah, but if you look
at his bank accounts, plain vanilla.

163
00:10:46,459 --> 00:10:48,335
Then eight months ago,
he opens a savings account

164
00:10:48,502 --> 00:10:51,505
and he gets once-monthly direct deposits
from some company in the Caymans.

165
00:10:51,630 --> 00:10:53,466
- 10 grand a pop.
- Shady.

166
00:10:54,925 --> 00:10:55,968
'Sup, ladies?

167
00:10:56,469 --> 00:10:57,636
- Hey.
- Agent Wade.

168
00:10:57,762 --> 00:10:59,513
Gang unit has a tip
on a counterfeiting ring

169
00:10:59,638 --> 00:11:01,766
running out of a garage
on west capitol.

170
00:11:02,099 --> 00:11:03,934
Rapid response team
is up for the bust.

171
00:11:04,101 --> 00:11:07,229
- Nice. Now?
- Roll call in 30.

172
00:11:07,480 --> 00:11:10,608
- I squared it with your cute boss.
- I'm in.

173
00:11:10,775 --> 00:11:14,862
- Uh, we have work to do.
- I'll have him back in a few hours, dad.

174
00:11:15,154 --> 00:11:18,491
Look, if you're the rapid response team
shouldn't you be sliding down fire poles

175
00:11:18,616 --> 00:11:20,326
not hanging around here
for 30 minutes?

176
00:11:24,121 --> 00:11:26,415
So Jeremy was making money
on the side.

177
00:11:26,540 --> 00:11:29,126
More than he was making
at Westerly Organics.

178
00:11:29,376 --> 00:11:31,170
Maybe that's the boss
he was fighting with.

179
00:11:31,587 --> 00:11:33,380
Except I can't figure out
who was paying him.

180
00:11:33,672 --> 00:11:36,217
A company in Romania that was
incorporated in the Caymans.

181
00:11:36,383 --> 00:11:37,426
Keep digging.

182
00:11:37,551 --> 00:11:39,345
We need to figure out
what was in that safe.

183
00:11:39,470 --> 00:11:40,638
Uh, what do you think
it was, drugs?

184
00:11:40,971 --> 00:11:44,600
He had too much respect for his work
to sell it in Ziploc baggies.

185
00:11:44,725 --> 00:11:45,768
Well, it wasn't cash.

186
00:11:45,893 --> 00:11:48,312
All the money deposited in his account's
still there except for 8 grand.

187
00:11:48,437 --> 00:11:49,772
Which he gave to his friend.

188
00:11:49,897 --> 00:11:52,817
Preliminary forensics report puts
the time of death around 6:00 a.m.

189
00:11:53,109 --> 00:11:56,612
No one had seen Jeremy for 12 hours.
Maybe he was moonlighting.

190
00:11:56,737 --> 00:11:59,323
- Might explain those deposits.
- Jane? Thoughts?

191
00:11:59,698 --> 00:12:01,450
- I'm puzzled.
- That's evidence.

192
00:12:01,575 --> 00:12:03,452
I want you to get it
to technical services

193
00:12:03,577 --> 00:12:06,038
and have it opened, on my desk
in the morning. Are we good?

194
00:12:06,163 --> 00:12:09,208
You seem a little obsessed
with evaluating our status.

195
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:12,336
Is it good, bad...
I mean, we're alive.

196
00:12:13,003 --> 00:12:14,338
The guy that made this is dead.

197
00:12:14,463 --> 00:12:16,382
By comparison, I think
we're doing very well.

198
00:12:16,757 --> 00:12:17,800
Tomorrow morning.

199
00:13:23,991 --> 00:13:25,743
Police! Hands in the air!

200
00:13:25,868 --> 00:13:27,745
Hands in the air!
Put them now!

201
00:13:28,037 --> 00:13:29,747
- Up against the wall!
- I got him.

202
00:13:30,039 --> 00:13:31,081
Hands in the air!

203
00:13:36,170 --> 00:13:39,673
- Kimball?
- Summer?

204
00:14:37,940 --> 00:14:39,900
- Hands in the air!
- Already done.

205
00:14:40,359 --> 00:14:41,402
Down on the ground!

206
00:14:43,028 --> 00:14:44,989
Well... do...uh...

207
00:14:45,614 --> 00:14:49,410
Uh, hands in the air or...or down
on the ground? I'm a little confused.

208
00:14:57,882 --> 00:15:00,426
- A tobacco laboratory?
- Well, I didn't know what it was.

209
00:15:00,551 --> 00:15:04,806
- Oh, so you just broke in?
- I left you a voicemail.

210
00:15:05,098 --> 00:15:06,516
How did you get the box open?

211
00:15:08,142 --> 00:15:10,686
Francesca Ehrlich
with JG Allen Tobacco.

212
00:15:10,812 --> 00:15:14,899
- This is Stuart Davis, my boss.
- Ah. Patrick Jane, consultant.

213
00:15:15,024 --> 00:15:16,734
This, uh, isn't my boss.

214
00:15:17,318 --> 00:15:21,531
- I am Teresa Lisbon, and I am his boss.
- So you're responsible for Mr. Jane

215
00:15:21,656 --> 00:15:24,367
compromising a top-secret
research facility?

216
00:15:24,492 --> 00:15:28,287
Jeremy had a key. What was he doing
with a key to a tobacco laboratory?

217
00:15:28,412 --> 00:15:32,875
Well, Jeremy is...
was... a freelance botanist.

218
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:36,254
He worked nights and weekends for us
about nine months?

219
00:15:36,379 --> 00:15:38,506
- Yeah.
- And what's your role here?

220
00:15:38,631 --> 00:15:41,300
I'm senior vice president
in charge of new products.

221
00:15:41,467 --> 00:15:43,803
New products?
Like, uh, the, uh...

222
00:15:45,179 --> 00:15:46,597
Mar...marijuana?

223
00:15:46,973 --> 00:15:51,894
Our analysis is that marijuana
will be decriminalized in our lifetime.

224
00:15:52,145 --> 00:15:55,439
And JG Allen will be
ready to embrace that market.

225
00:15:55,648 --> 00:15:58,609
Still a controversial area,
hence the secrecy.

226
00:15:58,734 --> 00:16:02,321
So you were having Jeremy Reese
grow you some corporate weed?

227
00:16:02,446 --> 00:16:06,492
He was developing a fungus-resistant
mass-producible marijuana strain.

228
00:16:06,701 --> 00:16:09,287
Project Turbo Wolf.
It was part of my portfolio.

229
00:16:09,787 --> 00:16:11,372
Project Turbo Wolf?

230
00:16:12,123 --> 00:16:14,333
Uh, we heard that Jeremy
was arguing with his boss.

231
00:16:14,458 --> 00:16:17,253
But it wasn't at his day job, was it?
It was you.

232
00:16:17,461 --> 00:16:21,757
Well, disagreements between researchers
and the product team are common.

233
00:16:22,175 --> 00:16:24,802
And there was a spirited
discussion a few days ago.

234
00:16:25,136 --> 00:16:29,807
We haven't seen Jeremy since.
And... how do I put this delicately?

235
00:16:30,141 --> 00:16:34,145
He had just harvested
his Turbo Wolf seeds.

236
00:16:34,520 --> 00:16:38,149
- And we can't seem to find them.
- That may be what was in the safe.

237
00:16:38,441 --> 00:16:39,483
What safe?

238
00:16:40,193 --> 00:16:44,071
Well, it seems someone
may have bogarted your stash.

239
00:16:44,280 --> 00:16:46,657
Well, we have to find those seeds.

240
00:16:47,533 --> 00:16:50,203
I mean, this project will be worth billions
to the company one day.

241
00:16:50,328 --> 00:16:52,371
- Billions? Wow.
- How much is it worth today?

242
00:16:52,496 --> 00:16:56,500
Well, I'm sure our competitors
would pay millions to acquire it.

243
00:16:56,626 --> 00:16:59,378
I'll bet you dollars to doughnuts
I can find your seeds.

244
00:16:59,503 --> 00:17:01,839
Let's start with a tour
of the lab, shall we?

245
00:17:04,133 --> 00:17:06,886
Looks like your marijuana
project is well funded.

246
00:17:07,011 --> 00:17:09,305
Well, it's not all
just marijuana, per se.

247
00:17:09,472 --> 00:17:12,099
We're also developing a range
of non-narcotic products.

248
00:17:12,308 --> 00:17:14,310
- Jeremy's desk, I imagine?
- Yes, sir.

249
00:17:14,435 --> 00:17:16,062
It's exactly as he left it.

250
00:17:28,908 --> 00:17:30,159
What's the story
with this box?

251
00:17:30,284 --> 00:17:33,120
Because whenever I touch it,
you blink rapidly.

252
00:17:35,998 --> 00:17:38,542
- Uh, nothing.
- Could you step back, Mr. Pierce?

253
00:17:38,668 --> 00:17:40,378
Thank you. Making me nervous.

254
00:17:52,014 --> 00:17:54,600
- That looks like a bug.
- Cool.

255
00:17:55,768 --> 00:17:59,021
- Summer Edgecombe. Wow.
- Yeah.

256
00:18:00,606 --> 00:18:02,775
That's not...
that's not yours, is it?

257
00:18:03,901 --> 00:18:07,613
No. She's in town for her wedding
to the baby's father.

258
00:18:07,989 --> 00:18:11,784
- Gonna be a pretty big deal, apparently.
- Wow.

259
00:18:12,451 --> 00:18:13,494
You said that.

260
00:18:14,078 --> 00:18:17,248
Yep. You dodged a bullet
when you let that one slip away.

261
00:18:24,630 --> 00:18:26,966
Oh, my God. Kimball.
Can you believe this?

262
00:18:27,091 --> 00:18:31,470
I'm in town for, like, five days and boom...
here we are. Like old times.

263
00:18:31,595 --> 00:18:32,638
Yeah. Just like.

264
00:18:33,931 --> 00:18:35,516
Summer, why didn't you call me
if you were in trouble?

265
00:18:36,392 --> 00:18:38,519
- I wasn't in trouble.
- Well, you are now.

266
00:18:38,769 --> 00:18:41,772
Oh, please. This?
You can make this go away.

267
00:18:41,981 --> 00:18:45,443
I'm a confidential informant.
I got juice in this building.

268
00:18:45,568 --> 00:18:48,029
Yeah, but you were in a garage
full of phony cash.

269
00:18:48,154 --> 00:18:49,488
No. No, no, no. No.

270
00:18:49,613 --> 00:18:51,657
Chuck said we were
just going for a ride.

271
00:18:51,991 --> 00:18:53,200
I was bored, so I went.

272
00:18:53,326 --> 00:18:55,745
And then he said he had
to stop by some friends'.

273
00:18:55,870 --> 00:18:59,123
And... well, when I got there,
I realized it was a bad deal.

274
00:18:59,332 --> 00:19:00,875
By then, it was too late.

275
00:19:02,168 --> 00:19:03,336
Eight months pregnant?

276
00:19:04,337 --> 00:19:06,255
How stupid do you think
I think you are?

277
00:19:06,422 --> 00:19:12,553
Uh, I swear, I have nothing to do
with this counterfeiting, or whatever.

278
00:19:12,678 --> 00:19:15,806
- Like, at all. Ask those guys.
- Well, they're not talking.

279
00:19:16,932 --> 00:19:18,851
Well, what are we gonna do?
The wedding's in two days.

280
00:19:18,976 --> 00:19:21,896
- I... I can't stay here.
- Your buddy Chuck could help.

281
00:19:22,688 --> 00:19:25,483
- You don't believe me.
- Doesn't matter what I believe.

282
00:19:26,108 --> 00:19:28,194
It matters to me, Kimball.

283
00:19:29,570 --> 00:19:33,074
I... I did what you said.
I went to Seattle. I got cleaned up.

284
00:19:33,199 --> 00:19:35,368
And... and Marshall, he's nice.

285
00:19:35,743 --> 00:19:38,204
He's... he's in real estate.
We have a house.

286
00:19:38,788 --> 00:19:42,625
That's good. And if this guy's
halfway decent, he'll understand.

287
00:19:42,792 --> 00:19:47,213
No, no, no. He can't know.
He thinks I'm at my sister's.

288
00:19:47,630 --> 00:19:51,258
Look, I love him.
But there's things about me...

289
00:19:53,010 --> 00:19:56,347
Please?
No one else is gonna help me.

290
00:20:03,562 --> 00:20:04,897
Tell me where to find Chuck.

291
00:20:08,192 --> 00:20:11,028
- You need to let her go.
- I knew this was coming.

292
00:20:11,153 --> 00:20:12,863
Look, I know the woman.
She was my C.I.

293
00:20:13,030 --> 00:20:14,615
I heard she was
a lot more than that.

294
00:20:15,157 --> 00:20:17,701
Look, she didn't do this.
She was just in the wrong place.

295
00:20:17,952 --> 00:20:20,496
Maybe she was in the right place
as a lookout.

296
00:20:20,621 --> 00:20:23,207
No. I know her.
And I believe her.

297
00:20:23,499 --> 00:20:26,669
This is my case.
I'm charging her.

298
00:20:30,005 --> 00:20:31,048
Now what?

299
00:20:31,757 --> 00:20:34,385
Cop to cop, you know
how this goes.

300
00:20:35,177 --> 00:20:37,346
Just give me what I need,
and let's get out of here.

301
00:20:41,600 --> 00:20:43,352
Davis won't say this,
but three days ago

302
00:20:43,477 --> 00:20:45,438
I was called upstairs
to restrain the kid.

303
00:20:46,021 --> 00:20:48,107
He's screaming, "it's my work."

304
00:20:49,108 --> 00:20:50,484
The project's completed, right?

305
00:20:51,026 --> 00:20:53,654
They wanna move the seeds
into secure storage.

306
00:20:55,448 --> 00:20:56,782
That's when the kid lost it.

307
00:20:57,700 --> 00:21:00,327
I'm gonna need access
to all the recordings you made.

308
00:21:00,953 --> 00:21:04,915
Then you're gonna need
a court order. Cop to cop.

309
00:21:13,215 --> 00:21:16,719
- Cho's awfully trusting.
- It's from the rapid response raid.

310
00:21:16,844 --> 00:21:18,637
It's counterfeit.
There's bags and bags of it.

311
00:21:19,013 --> 00:21:21,432
Bags and bags of it?
Imagine that.

312
00:21:22,766 --> 00:21:24,185
You like the security
guard for the murder?

313
00:21:24,727 --> 00:21:27,480
Pierce? Eh.
He's well trained, but a lapdog.

314
00:21:27,855 --> 00:21:29,899
Lifestyle's too comfy
to risk murder.

315
00:21:30,024 --> 00:21:32,109
That may be true,
but I'm gonna hold him

316
00:21:32,234 --> 00:21:34,528
until I can get a warrant
to search the tobacco lab.

317
00:21:34,987 --> 00:21:38,073
- Did you check the security logs yet?
- Yeah. On the night of his murder

318
00:21:38,199 --> 00:21:41,160
Jeremy swiped into
JG Allen Tobacco at 8:00 p.m.

319
00:21:41,327 --> 00:21:42,369
That makes sense.

320
00:21:42,495 --> 00:21:44,538
They were planning to take
his work to cold storage

321
00:21:44,663 --> 00:21:46,832
so he went to the lab
to take back his seeds.

322
00:21:46,957 --> 00:21:49,418
Seeds worth millions of dollars.
Certainly worth killing for.

323
00:21:49,543 --> 00:21:52,463
Find the seeds,
you'll find the killer.

324
00:21:52,630 --> 00:21:55,716
Grace, do you have
any of Jeremy's notebooks from the lab?

325
00:21:55,841 --> 00:21:59,637
Uh, yeah. Sure.
Right here.

326
00:22:00,262 --> 00:22:02,932
A lot of it's gibberish.
Like a code?

327
00:22:03,516 --> 00:22:06,560
There's a long tradition of scientists
that disguise their thoughts in code.

328
00:22:06,685 --> 00:22:08,437
- Let me see.
- You have an idea?

329
00:22:08,604 --> 00:22:09,939
Eh, more of a notion.

330
00:22:10,064 --> 00:22:12,775
We need to interview every
employee from the tobacco lab.

331
00:22:12,900 --> 00:22:15,903
Rigsby, run background checks.
And let's get the execs back here.

332
00:22:42,471 --> 00:22:43,514
CBI, ma'am.

333
00:22:44,098 --> 00:22:46,517
We have information
Chuck Calloway's been staying here.

334
00:22:46,725 --> 00:22:47,768
I don't know any Chuck.

335
00:22:47,893 --> 00:22:50,062
Really? You're his sister,
so how's that work

336
00:23:11,625 --> 00:23:13,043
- You got it?
- I got it!

337
00:23:13,877 --> 00:23:15,629
- Police brutality, man!
- Not yet!

338
00:23:16,714 --> 00:23:18,507
- Give me your arms.
- I'm not resisting!

339
00:23:27,474 --> 00:23:29,560
- Pierce, isn't it?
- What do you want?

340
00:23:29,977 --> 00:23:33,564
What do I want? I want your professional
opinion if that's okay.

341
00:23:33,939 --> 00:23:37,776
Uh, I found this note
in Jeremy's apartment.

342
00:23:37,901 --> 00:23:42,072
That's actually a copy.
You know that name? Olivier Gans?

343
00:23:43,324 --> 00:23:44,533
No. Should I?

344
00:23:44,700 --> 00:23:47,411
Ah, he's a pharmaceutical
company buyer, middle man.

345
00:23:49,997 --> 00:23:53,709
- You think Jeremy knew him?
- Well, we don't know.

346
00:23:53,834 --> 00:23:56,629
As you can see, the rest
of the note is in code

347
00:23:57,338 --> 00:24:00,716
- and I can't... I can't crack it, so...
- Well, maybe I could.

348
00:24:01,717 --> 00:24:04,219
Sure. Be my guest.

349
00:24:05,554 --> 00:24:07,806
If anything pops out,
just let me know.

350
00:24:14,980 --> 00:24:16,231
Who's Olivier Gans?

351
00:24:16,523 --> 00:24:18,359
Uh, I don't know.
Made him up.

352
00:24:26,915 --> 00:24:29,209
- How do you know Summer?
- Oh, we're friends.

353
00:24:30,210 --> 00:24:32,253
She and my cousin hooked up once.
Maybe more than once.

354
00:24:32,379 --> 00:24:33,630
- A few times, actually.
- I got it.

355
00:24:34,506 --> 00:24:35,966
Now what were you two doing
at that warehouse?

356
00:24:45,183 --> 00:24:47,560
It's okay.
No one knows you're here.

357
00:24:53,733 --> 00:24:54,901
Shall we go another round?

358
00:24:57,445 --> 00:24:59,823
I did some work for these guys.

359
00:25:00,949 --> 00:25:03,743
I'd buy some stuff with their bills
and then return it for cash.

360
00:25:04,911 --> 00:25:06,454
I don't care about
your criminal offenses.

361
00:25:06,955 --> 00:25:08,540
I want to know what Summer Edgecombe
was doing there.

362
00:25:09,082 --> 00:25:13,545
Oh, dude... These are not good people.
Okay? They...they scare me.

363
00:25:14,796 --> 00:25:17,007
I was dropping off some cash,
but I was a little light

364
00:25:17,632 --> 00:25:20,010
and I thought they'd be chill
if I brought a pregnant lady.

365
00:25:21,678 --> 00:25:23,722
You're an idiot, Chuck.
And I need names.

366
00:25:24,097 --> 00:25:25,724
Everything you know
about that counterfeit ring.

367
00:25:28,018 --> 00:25:31,479
- And if I can't remember?
- Do you need another enemy?

368
00:25:33,565 --> 00:25:37,485
Researchers consistently fail to understand
the importance of their own work.

369
00:25:38,028 --> 00:25:39,654
Is that why you spy on people?

370
00:25:40,155 --> 00:25:44,701
We have every right to maintain
operational oversight of our employees...

371
00:25:44,909 --> 00:25:47,746
- freelancers included.
- Maybe legally speaking.

372
00:25:47,912 --> 00:25:50,582
- How do you feel about it, Francesca?
- Well...

373
00:25:50,707 --> 00:25:55,503
I chose not to inform Francesca
of my instructions to Pierce.

374
00:25:56,087 --> 00:25:58,089
So you don't trust
your executives, either?

375
00:25:58,757 --> 00:26:00,717
I wonder if there's hidden
microphones in your office.

376
00:26:00,842 --> 00:26:03,928
There's not.
There's not, Francesca.

377
00:26:04,262 --> 00:26:06,639
- Of course.
- We'll find out soon enough.

378
00:26:06,765 --> 00:26:09,934
We're working on getting a warrant
to obtain your security recordings.

379
00:26:10,894 --> 00:26:14,647
Uh, excuse me. Olivier Gans...
are you familiar with him?

380
00:26:15,231 --> 00:26:18,777
Big-time underworld pharmaceutical buyer.
Very clandestine.

381
00:26:21,404 --> 00:26:22,697
That looks like Jeremy's writing.

382
00:26:23,073 --> 00:26:24,574
Yeah, we found it
among his papers.

383
00:26:24,699 --> 00:26:29,037
We have reason to believe that he
and this Gans guy were in contact.

384
00:26:29,329 --> 00:26:31,289
And you ask if I trust my people?

385
00:26:31,539 --> 00:26:33,541
Do you think Jeremy was
shopping around Turbo Wolf?

386
00:26:33,833 --> 00:26:37,003
Well, maybe. This guy's very connected.
He deals all over the globe.

387
00:26:37,462 --> 00:26:40,465
- And this code?
- Well, yeah. We're stumped.

388
00:26:40,590 --> 00:26:42,467
Maybe somebody on your team
can get to the bottom of it.

389
00:26:42,592 --> 00:26:44,719
- When is Jeremy's memorial?
- Tomorrow morning.

390
00:26:47,931 --> 00:26:48,973
Sorry for the intrusion.

391
00:26:56,147 --> 00:26:58,108
D.D.A. Ardiles.
Charles Calloway.

392
00:27:00,110 --> 00:27:04,239
- Agent Cho, always a pleasure.
- You can call me Chuck.

393
00:27:05,115 --> 00:27:07,283
Ah, I think I will. Chuck.

394
00:27:08,701 --> 00:27:12,247
So... we looked into some
of the names you gave Agent Cho.

395
00:27:12,372 --> 00:27:14,249
If these claims hold water

396
00:27:14,374 --> 00:27:16,626
this counterfeiting ring
you're hooked into is quite extensive.

397
00:27:16,751 --> 00:27:20,130
Well, dude
these guys run California, okay?

398
00:27:20,839 --> 00:27:23,258
- And you're prepared to inform on them?
- Yeah.

399
00:27:24,467 --> 00:27:26,386
Well, I mean,
for the right price.

400
00:27:29,055 --> 00:27:32,767
- Let me guess. Full immunity.
- I can't do time, man.

401
00:27:33,893 --> 00:27:37,939
- Hey.
- Oh, yeah. Uh...

402
00:27:39,607 --> 00:27:41,442
Uh, Summer Edgecombe...

403
00:27:42,902 --> 00:27:46,656
- I only come on board if she walks.
- Now the picture comes into focus.

404
00:27:46,781 --> 00:27:47,907
Look, Summer wasn't involved.

405
00:27:48,032 --> 00:27:49,576
She was just there
when the raid went down.

406
00:27:49,701 --> 00:27:52,036
Wasn't Summer Edgecombe
a former C.I. herself?

407
00:27:52,162 --> 00:27:54,873
She proved to be an asset
to the CBI on many occasions. Yeah.

408
00:27:55,874 --> 00:27:56,916
Nothing else?

409
00:28:01,963 --> 00:28:03,006
You and Summer?

410
00:28:05,216 --> 00:28:06,426
Look, are you gonna make
a deal or not?

411
00:28:07,177 --> 00:28:08,386
It's always something
with you guys.

412
00:28:09,637 --> 00:28:12,432
You know what? I wasn't looking forward
to prosecuting a pregnant woman anyhow.

413
00:28:14,058 --> 00:28:17,103
Welcome aboard, Chuck.
We'll draw up the paperwork.

414
00:28:53,598 --> 00:28:56,851
Say what you will about hippies.
They throw a good funeral.

415
00:28:57,435 --> 00:28:59,896
Sure, if classical guitar
is your bag.

416
00:29:01,356 --> 00:29:03,066
So did you get that puzzle box open?

417
00:29:03,358 --> 00:29:05,944
- Yes. Magnets.
- Magnets.

418
00:29:06,527 --> 00:29:08,112
He would have loved
that you figured that out.

419
00:29:08,988 --> 00:29:11,032
He didn't have a lot of people
that could play at his level.

420
00:29:11,574 --> 00:29:13,534
Yeah, well, that's a curse
some of us live with.

421
00:29:14,744 --> 00:29:15,870
Uh, excuse me, please.

422
00:29:23,169 --> 00:29:24,796
Hey, nice of you to come.

423
00:29:25,171 --> 00:29:26,589
Well, I had some business
in the area.

424
00:29:26,714 --> 00:29:29,008
Thought I'd stop by, show my respects.

425
00:29:29,592 --> 00:29:31,511
Some business?
You have a lead?

426
00:29:33,471 --> 00:29:35,265
Uh, it's okay.
You can trust Carson.

427
00:29:35,890 --> 00:29:40,853
Well, we have a...
confidential but, uh, very solid lead.

428
00:29:43,273 --> 00:29:47,986
- Olivier Gans? Who's that?
- He's a big-time pharmaceutical buyer.

429
00:29:48,361 --> 00:29:52,156
Uh, shady character, deep pockets
smoke-filled rooms.

430
00:29:53,074 --> 00:29:55,326
Was Jeremy trying to sell our strains?

431
00:29:56,744 --> 00:30:00,081
- Did you know about this?
- What? No. No, I swear.

432
00:30:00,206 --> 00:30:03,167
We'll find out soon enough
when we can crack Jeremy's code.

433
00:30:03,501 --> 00:30:08,047
Uh, please excuse me.
Hi, Lisbon.

434
00:30:08,423 --> 00:30:11,301
I don't know what game you're playing
but I think you're getting carried away

435
00:30:11,426 --> 00:30:13,636
- with this Olivier Gans thing.
- How so?

436
00:30:13,886 --> 00:30:16,431
I... I just got a call from the vice
President of something or other

437
00:30:16,556 --> 00:30:17,890
of JG Allen Enterprises.

438
00:30:18,016 --> 00:30:20,768
- They're up in arms about Gans.
- "Really?"

439
00:30:20,935 --> 00:30:23,146
They think he's trying
to steal Turbo Wolf.

440
00:30:23,438 --> 00:30:25,398
Well, he hasn't yet,
but he might soon.

441
00:30:26,107 --> 00:30:30,111
- We should meet at his hotel.
- The imaginary man has a hotel?

442
00:30:30,403 --> 00:30:33,197
- That's right.
- Is this hotel imaginary, too?

443
00:30:33,698 --> 00:30:35,074
Oh, no, very real.

444
00:30:35,325 --> 00:30:39,579
It's, uh, the Vista in Clearlake.
I'll meet you there in an hour. Bye.

445
00:30:44,751 --> 00:30:47,086
{\an8}"Jeremy was killed for his seeds."

446
00:30:47,337 --> 00:30:48,713
But the killer's faced
with a problem.

447
00:30:49,005 --> 00:30:51,716
Who wants to pay millions of dollars
for a handful of seeds?

448
00:30:52,091 --> 00:30:55,094
- Somebody who knows their value.
- Yeah, well, that's a very limited market

449
00:30:55,219 --> 00:30:56,721
and with a murder investigation afoot

450
00:30:56,846 --> 00:30:58,806
I am betting that the killer
is desperate enough

451
00:30:58,931 --> 00:31:01,059
to sell now
and get out of town.

452
00:31:01,309 --> 00:31:04,771
- So you made up a fake buyer?
- Olivier Gans.

453
00:31:05,063 --> 00:31:06,898
And the rest of the note
is Caesar code.

454
00:31:07,065 --> 00:31:10,068
Exactly the kind of thing that Jeremy
would use to hide his information.

455
00:31:10,985 --> 00:31:13,988
It translates to "3:00 p.m.
Friday at the Vista."

456
00:31:14,864 --> 00:31:15,907
Who's gonna figure that out?

457
00:31:16,199 --> 00:31:17,825
Whoever has millions
of dollars at stake.

458
00:31:17,992 --> 00:31:19,911
Assuming they do,
we can't arrest somebody

459
00:31:20,036 --> 00:31:22,246
- for knocking on a door.
- I'm well aware of that.

460
00:31:22,455 --> 00:31:24,749
That's why we're gonna get them
to sell us the merchandise.

461
00:31:25,041 --> 00:31:26,584
Olivier? You ready?

462
00:31:28,378 --> 00:31:30,088
Uh, yeah, lose the tie.

463
00:31:30,213 --> 00:31:33,216
- I don't think Olivier Gans is a tie guy.
- Okay, you got it.

464
00:31:35,426 --> 00:31:38,638
See that face? That's exactly why
I didn't tell you the plan.

465
00:31:39,097 --> 00:31:40,973
- It's not a good plan.
- It's a good enough plan

466
00:31:41,099 --> 00:31:43,267
and Rigsby's wearing
one of those ear thingies.

467
00:31:43,393 --> 00:31:45,561
I will talk him through the con.
He'll be fine.

468
00:31:45,728 --> 00:31:47,188
What if the killer
doesn't show up?

469
00:31:48,564 --> 00:31:50,233
Then we go back
to old-fashioned police work.

470
00:31:50,691 --> 00:31:52,568
But this way, we get to enjoy
some room service.

471
00:31:52,693 --> 00:31:53,736
Please, have a seat.

472
00:32:04,831 --> 00:32:07,500
- Enough of the cheese.
- "I'm hungry."

473
00:32:09,460 --> 00:32:12,296
- Jane, it's 3:15.
- Patience.

474
00:32:16,926 --> 00:32:19,929
Hello?
Yeah. Send him up.

475
00:32:25,601 --> 00:32:26,644
"On the way."

476
00:32:33,901 --> 00:32:35,778
- Mr. Gans, is it?
- And who are you?

477
00:32:36,696 --> 00:32:39,449
Jeremy couldn't be here.
You'll conduct business with me today.

478
00:32:39,991 --> 00:32:43,494
- I'm Francesca.
- Yeah. Come in.

479
00:32:44,454 --> 00:32:47,081
Once she makes her offer,
you sweep in and make your arrest.

480
00:32:47,331 --> 00:32:48,916
If she makes the offer.

481
00:32:50,084 --> 00:32:52,545
- How do I know you're not a cop?
- "Easy there, tiger."

482
00:32:52,670 --> 00:32:54,922
"Chicken's already in the coop.
Let's not overdo it."

483
00:32:56,007 --> 00:32:58,718
The cops don't have the real deal.
I do.

484
00:32:59,802 --> 00:33:01,137
There's only one
Turbo Wolf.

485
00:33:04,348 --> 00:33:08,227
- Would you like to sample the product?
- Yeah. Yeah, sure.

486
00:33:15,693 --> 00:33:17,028
Get her to show you
the seeds.

487
00:33:24,494 --> 00:33:27,205
That's nice.
Really smooth.

488
00:33:28,122 --> 00:33:31,000
It's a 60/40
Indica dominant hybrid

489
00:33:31,292 --> 00:33:34,170
ideal for commercial production
of smokable applications

490
00:33:34,295 --> 00:33:37,507
as well as for pills, oral sprays,
and dermal patches.

491
00:33:39,133 --> 00:33:42,261
Strong.
And, uh, where are the seeds?

492
00:33:42,678 --> 00:33:43,721
Right here.

493
00:33:44,555 --> 00:33:45,598
I'm going in.

494
00:33:49,227 --> 00:33:51,312
- Who's that?
- Uh, it's not us.

495
00:33:51,896 --> 00:33:55,650
- "Just... just answer it."
- Y-you just stay there. I got this.

496
00:34:01,405 --> 00:34:04,450
- Hi. Uh, you're Gans?
- Depends.

497
00:34:05,034 --> 00:34:06,536
You're looking for Turbo Wolf, right?

498
00:34:09,205 --> 00:34:11,457
Uh, could you pass the cheese plate?
This is getting good.

499
00:34:15,767 --> 00:34:16,810
Get in.

500
00:34:18,078 --> 00:34:19,704
They can't both have
Turbo Wolf.

501
00:34:20,247 --> 00:34:22,582
No, but one of them
killed Jeremy for it.

502
00:34:22,874 --> 00:34:24,167
All right,
then I'm pulling the plug.

503
00:34:24,292 --> 00:34:26,002
We can bring them in
and talk to them.

504
00:34:26,711 --> 00:34:29,673
Pish posh. He's doing a great job.
Let's just see what happens.

505
00:34:30,215 --> 00:34:33,385
- What's she doing here?
- Me? What are you doing here?

506
00:34:35,011 --> 00:34:37,889
You guys know each other?
That's nice.

507
00:34:38,556 --> 00:34:42,936
- The pot business is fairly small.
- Though I'm in the pot business, and...

508
00:34:44,145 --> 00:34:45,188
I don't know either of you.

509
00:34:47,023 --> 00:34:48,066
Am I talking loud?

510
00:34:48,775 --> 00:34:50,819
You're doing fine, Rigsby.
What we need to figure out...

511
00:34:50,944 --> 00:34:52,320
the story with Matthew Gold.

512
00:34:53,822 --> 00:34:54,864
Please...

513
00:34:56,908 --> 00:34:57,951
Oh, I know what to do.

514
00:34:58,535 --> 00:35:01,788
- You do?
- Maybe. We divide and conquer

515
00:35:02,455 --> 00:35:03,665
Give them a chance
to split the money.

516
00:35:03,915 --> 00:35:06,042
Rigsby, we're gonna play
a little game.

517
00:35:06,543 --> 00:35:07,961
It's called "repeat after me."

518
00:35:09,004 --> 00:35:10,797
"I can understand you're nervous..."

519
00:35:12,132 --> 00:35:13,341
I understand you're nervous.

520
00:35:13,967 --> 00:35:15,510
"You're both trying
to sell a dead man's work."

521
00:35:15,927 --> 00:35:18,763
You're both trying to sell
a dead man's product.

522
00:35:20,515 --> 00:35:21,558
Here's the thing.

523
00:35:22,309 --> 00:35:24,477
My client just wants results.

524
00:35:24,811 --> 00:35:27,772
"They're not interested
in provenance."

525
00:35:28,273 --> 00:35:31,443
They're not concerned
with provolone... provenance.

526
00:35:31,568 --> 00:35:34,070
They... they're not
concerned with provenance.

527
00:35:34,654 --> 00:35:36,072
They don't care
where it came from.

528
00:35:36,197 --> 00:35:38,908
"You both claim to have
Turbo Wolf to sell me."

529
00:35:39,492 --> 00:35:41,828
You both claim that you're
selling Turbo Wolf.

530
00:35:43,538 --> 00:35:44,581
Only one of you is right.

531
00:35:45,081 --> 00:35:47,042
"Now I don't have all day
to hash this out..."

532
00:35:47,500 --> 00:35:48,543
So here's what I'm gonna do.

533
00:35:52,130 --> 00:35:53,465
I have 4 million dollars.

534
00:35:58,219 --> 00:36:00,263
"That's the price that
Jeremy and I discussed."

535
00:36:02,682 --> 00:36:03,725
"Now..."

536
00:36:03,892 --> 00:36:07,103
I'm willing to buy both your seeds...

537
00:36:07,312 --> 00:36:09,439
"And you guys can split the money.
Or..."

538
00:36:12,984 --> 00:36:14,027
Or...

539
00:36:14,861 --> 00:36:16,821
One of you can prove
that you have the real deal.

540
00:36:18,406 --> 00:36:19,491
And you get it all.

541
00:36:22,243 --> 00:36:25,288
$2 million each?
I can live on that.

542
00:36:29,209 --> 00:36:32,879
I'm sure you can but I didn't
come here for half of anything.

543
00:36:33,004 --> 00:36:34,964
What are you talking about?
Let's take the deal.

544
00:36:35,423 --> 00:36:36,466
Why?

545
00:36:37,425 --> 00:36:41,513
I don't know what you have
but my product is authentic.

546
00:36:41,638 --> 00:36:43,264
I hired the team that grew it.

547
00:36:45,683 --> 00:36:48,478
- You poached a kid that I trained.
- Thanks for the head start.

548
00:36:48,978 --> 00:36:50,647
We're getting into the weeds here.
I'm on it.

549
00:36:51,231 --> 00:36:54,859
- "Okay, let's not get personal."
- Let's not get, uh, personal.

550
00:36:55,568 --> 00:36:56,611
Let's... have some cheese, huh?

551
00:36:58,905 --> 00:37:02,742
If we can't make a deal
I'm gonna walk out of here right now.

552
00:37:03,243 --> 00:37:08,540
Fine. I'm not splitting.
You're in over your head, Matthew.

553
00:37:09,082 --> 00:37:11,793
All right, yeah. Yeah.
She might have grown this stuff

554
00:37:12,127 --> 00:37:15,171
but Jeremy came to me when she tried
to put his product on ice.

555
00:37:15,880 --> 00:37:16,923
I convinced...

556
00:37:19,092 --> 00:37:21,428
I convinced him to steal
his seeds from the lab.

557
00:37:22,053 --> 00:37:25,723
The same seeds I took from his safe.
She's got nothing.

558
00:37:27,350 --> 00:37:29,561
- Oh, my God. You killed him?
- Yeah, what?

559
00:37:29,727 --> 00:37:31,229
You need me to spell it out
for you?

560
00:37:32,313 --> 00:37:34,274
I'm not walking away
with half now. I want it all!

561
00:37:34,732 --> 00:37:37,152
- Okay, hands in the air!
- What the hell are you talking about?

562
00:37:38,778 --> 00:37:40,905
- Um, I... I mean, nobody move!
- Whoa.

563
00:37:41,030 --> 00:37:42,657
I'm a cop.
Just... nobody move. See?

564
00:37:42,782 --> 00:37:45,118
Matthew Gold,
you're under arrest for murder!

565
00:37:45,660 --> 00:37:47,787
- Rigsby, hook her up, too.
- Wait... for what?

566
00:37:47,912 --> 00:37:50,331
- Industrial espionage, to start with.
- Yeah, I got it.

567
00:37:52,292 --> 00:37:54,711
Nope. I'm...
I'm gonna sit down.

568
00:37:55,253 --> 00:37:58,089
Let me see your hands.
Now! You, too.

569
00:37:59,424 --> 00:38:02,218
- Boss, is it okay if I sit down?
- You are sitting down, Rigsby.

570
00:38:05,472 --> 00:38:06,556
I felt betrayed, you know?

571
00:38:07,974 --> 00:38:09,851
That he'd go behind
my back like that.

572
00:38:11,478 --> 00:38:12,562
And for a tobacco company?

573
00:38:13,605 --> 00:38:17,317
Then I figured
they gave him resources I couldn't.

574
00:38:17,692 --> 00:38:21,070
"And when they told him they were putting
his project on hold for who knows how long"

575
00:38:21,946 --> 00:38:24,866
- "you convinced him to steal it back."
- "I told him with..."

576
00:38:25,992 --> 00:38:28,286
my reputation, we could
offer his strain to the world.

577
00:38:29,829 --> 00:38:30,997
But that was never your intent.

578
00:38:33,625 --> 00:38:35,043
I should be rich by now, you know?

579
00:38:36,878 --> 00:38:38,087
But this whole industry, the...

580
00:38:38,963 --> 00:38:40,381
the rules change every day.

581
00:38:41,174 --> 00:38:43,134
I knew when he came to me, I...

582
00:38:44,010 --> 00:38:45,261
I could take this stuff, you know?

583
00:38:46,554 --> 00:38:47,972
I knew he'd be at the gym.

584
00:38:51,100 --> 00:38:52,727
"I tried to tell myself
the gun was just there"

585
00:38:52,894 --> 00:38:54,145
"so he'd give me
the keys to the safe."

586
00:38:55,730 --> 00:38:58,525
"But... I knew."

587
00:39:01,319 --> 00:39:02,445
I had already gone too far.

588
00:39:03,238 --> 00:39:05,532
I gotta hand it to her.
She played you all.

589
00:39:06,324 --> 00:39:08,701
- What are you talking about?
- Francesca.

590
00:39:09,911 --> 00:39:10,954
She just confessed.

591
00:39:12,121 --> 00:39:15,041
Once Jeremy's work
went into cold storage

592
00:39:15,166 --> 00:39:17,919
she knew he'd sneak back
into the lab to steal his seeds.

593
00:39:19,087 --> 00:39:20,505
"That's why she switched
them out first"

594
00:39:20,630 --> 00:39:22,549
"so she could sell them
for herself on the black market."

595
00:39:24,801 --> 00:39:27,178
You mean Francesca had
the Turbo Wolf all along?

596
00:39:28,388 --> 00:39:29,430
Yep.

597
00:39:30,181 --> 00:39:33,017
You killed your protege for a handful
of worthless hemp seeds.

598
00:39:40,108 --> 00:39:41,150
Agent Wade.

599
00:39:45,280 --> 00:39:48,241
- I hope this Summer girl's worth it.
- It's not like that.

600
00:39:48,866 --> 00:39:49,909
- Really?
- Yeah.

601
00:39:50,827 --> 00:39:52,996
Look, I should have come to you
before going to the D.D.A.'s office.

602
00:39:53,538 --> 00:39:56,291
- I would have shut you down.
- I wouldn't have blamed you.

603
00:39:57,917 --> 00:40:02,213
- So you're not mad?
- Mad? This is about trust.

604
00:40:02,714 --> 00:40:04,215
I put together a rapid response team.

605
00:40:04,340 --> 00:40:06,175
I gotta know we're watching
each other's back.

606
00:40:06,301 --> 00:40:08,928
- You went behind mine.
- It'll never happen again.

607
00:40:10,888 --> 00:40:11,931
That's for sure.

608
00:40:29,240 --> 00:40:30,283
Kimball.

609
00:40:31,326 --> 00:40:33,119
- Hey.
- Hi.

610
00:40:34,579 --> 00:40:36,289
What'd you think, I was
just gonna disappear?

611
00:40:36,414 --> 00:40:39,208
- I wouldn't put it past you.
- Well, you saved the wedding.

612
00:40:39,667 --> 00:40:41,210
And this was on the way, so...

613
00:40:41,336 --> 00:40:43,671
- Uh, babe?
- Oh, Marshall, come here.

614
00:40:43,880 --> 00:40:46,716
I want you to meet my friend,
Agent Cho.

615
00:40:47,216 --> 00:40:48,801
Agent Cho, this is Marshall.

616
00:40:49,385 --> 00:40:50,970
- Uh, the fiancé.
- It's nice to meet you.

617
00:40:51,137 --> 00:40:53,014
You got any firemen to say
good-bye to, or what?

618
00:40:53,806 --> 00:40:56,267
Uh, no offense, buddy,
but, uh, we got a thing.

619
00:40:56,392 --> 00:40:57,685
Just give me a minute,
would you?

620
00:41:01,856 --> 00:41:04,108
He's a good guy.
He really is.

621
00:41:05,985 --> 00:41:08,404
We're heading down to Pismo
for the honeymoon.

622
00:41:08,529 --> 00:41:10,281
- It's nice down there.
- Yeah.

623
00:41:11,658 --> 00:41:12,700
Thank you, Kimball.

624
00:41:14,577 --> 00:41:16,412
I promise I won't bug you anymore.

625
00:41:18,122 --> 00:41:19,165
I'm gonna be good.

626
00:41:21,292 --> 00:41:23,252
Good-bye, Summer.
Good luck.

627
00:41:25,213 --> 00:41:27,757
I'd kiss you, but Marshall
gets crazy jealous.

628
00:41:51,489 --> 00:41:53,491
This is so annoying.
I can't find my keys.

629
00:41:54,325 --> 00:41:55,910
- Did you check your desk?
- Of course I...

630
00:42:15,430 --> 00:42:17,432
Oh. Here they are.
Silly me.

631
00:42:18,641 --> 00:42:20,435
You keep a hammer
in your desk.

632
00:42:20,935 --> 00:42:22,645
You only think you know
everything about me.

